window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"][10]={"university_computer_science_en:M10-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M10-L1 – Networks and practical IT security<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Bachelor’s: Networks and databases<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Classification:<\/strong> Optional Bachelor’s elective topic\n(M10-L1)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Reading path:<\/strong> M1-L6 (Networks and security), M2-L5\n(Cloud networks) and M4-L3 (Security and data protection) provide the\nfoundations. This lesson explores the technology and practical risk\nassessment in greater depth. It can be read without exercises, scans,\naccounts, programs or installations. <strong>Mathematical\nbridge:<\/strong> Sets and propositional logic are explored in more depth\nin University M1-L1, graphs in University M2-L1 and conditional\nprobability in School M1-L22.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why a network is more than a cable<\/h2>\n<p>A computer can process data on its own. Once several computers\ncommunicate, additional tasks arise: they must find the recipient,\nforward messages along the way, detect failures and decide whom to\ntrust. A local file can be read directly. A file on another computer, by\ncontrast, requires at least a connection, an agreed language and an\naccess decision.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>network<\/strong> is not merely the physical cable or\nwireless network. It includes devices, rules, addresses, protocols, name\nresolution, connections to other networks and security boundaries. Two\nprograms can communicate on the same computer using network mechanisms;\nconversely, an application with many servers can internally appear to be\na single service.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Laptop on home network\n   | Wi-Fi / Ethernet\n   v\nRouter / internet access\n   | Forwarding through several networks\n   v\nPublic web server\n   | Verified application interface\n   v\nInternal database (not publicly accessible)<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Guiding question:<\/strong> Which participant may send which\nmessage to which destination – and what happens if a device, credential\nor network segment is compromised? Network security therefore begins\nwith designing communication relationships and does not end with\nswitching on a firewall.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Layers and protocols: How data finds its way<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>protocol<\/strong> is a collection of agreed rules: who\nsends what information and when, what a response means and how errors\nare handled. The internet uses a family of protocols built upon one\nanother. A simplified four-layer model helps to put them into\ncontext:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Perspective<\/th>\n<th>Typical technology<\/th>\n<th>Question<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Application<\/td>\n<td>HTTP, DNS, email protocols<\/td>\n<td>What information should be exchanged?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transport<\/td>\n<td>TCP, UDP, QUIC<\/td>\n<td>Which program endpoint? How reliable or fast?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Internet<\/td>\n<td>IPv4, IPv6, IP routing<\/td>\n<td>To which logical network address? Through which networks?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Network access<\/td>\n<td>Ethernet, Wi-Fi<\/td>\n<td>How is a data frame transmitted on the directly reachable\nnetwork?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>HTTP<\/strong> (<em>Hypertext Transfer Protocol<\/em>) governs\nweb requests and responses, among other things. <strong>DNS<\/strong>\n(<em>Domain Name System<\/em>) associates names such as\n<code>beispiel.invalid<\/code> with the information needed to establish a\nconnection. <strong>IP<\/strong> (<em>Internet Protocol<\/em>) forwards\npackets between networks. <strong>TCP<\/strong> (<em>Transmission Control\nProtocol<\/em>) provides an ordered, reliable byte stream.\n<strong>UDP<\/strong> (<em>User Datagram Protocol<\/em>) transports\nindividual datagrams without the same built-in delivery and ordering\nguarantees. <strong>QUIC<\/strong> combines a transport-like approach\nover UDP with encryption and multiple-stream capabilities; HTTP/3 uses\nQUIC.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Encapsulation<\/strong> means that a layer embeds the data of\nthe higher layer into its own structure. This is comparable to a letter\ntransported in an envelope whose address differs from an internal order\nnumber.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Application data:  &quot;Retrieve the home page&quot;\nTransport:         Associated connection / port\nInternet:          Source and destination IP addresses\nNetwork access:    Frame to the next reachable device\n\nIntermediate routers replace the frame for the next network.\nThe HTTP message remains logically the same.<\/code><\/pre>\n<h3 id=\"switch-router-and-access-point\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Switch, router and access\npoint<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>switch<\/strong> connects participants within a local\nnetwork or so-called Layer 2 segment and frequently uses <strong>MAC\naddresses<\/strong> (<em>Media Access Control<\/em>, local interface\naddresses) for this purpose. A <strong>router<\/strong> connects\ndifferent IP networks and chooses a next forwarding step. A Wi-Fi\n<strong>access point<\/strong> provides wireless access to a network;\nhome routers often combine these functions in one device.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>MAC address<\/strong> is not the global internet address and\nis not reliable proof of a person’s identity. Similarly, an <strong>IP\naddress<\/strong> is initially a technical address, not evidence of an\nauthorised person. With <strong>NAT<\/strong> (<em>Network Address\nTranslation<\/em>), address information is translated when passing\nbetween networks. NAT can indirectly restrict certain connections but is\n<strong>not a substitute for an intentionally configured security\nrule<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"dns-and-the-question-of-what-a-name-proves\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>DNS and the question\nof what a name proves<\/h3>\n<p>An address such as <code>https://lernbeispiel.example<\/code> is\nreadable by humans. Before contacting a service, one or more name\nresolutions normally occur. <strong>DNS responses are not equivalent to\na security assurance:<\/strong> domain resolution specifies where a\nrequest should technically go. Whether the connection to the expected\nservice is authentic and confidential must be verified separately.\nDNSSEC can support the authenticity of DNS data; it does not encrypt the\nquery itself. Encrypted DNS transport methods, in turn, do not\nautomatically resolve the question of whether a website is\ntrustworthy.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Typical misconception:<\/strong> “I entered the correct\ninternet address, so all the following data is secure.” Different\nverification steps exist between the name, address, certificate, server\nconfiguration, application permissions and user login.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>IP addresses, networks and the significance of a subnet\nboundary<\/h2>\n<p><strong>IPv4<\/strong> uses 32-bit addresses, often written as four\ndecimal numbers (<code>192.0.2.1<\/code>). <strong>IPv6<\/strong> uses\n128-bit addresses and a different notation (<code>2001:db8::1<\/code>).\nThe example ranges here are intended for documentation and are not real\nreachable endpoints. Both protocols provide addressing and forwarding,\nbut payload encryption does not arise automatically from using IPv6.<\/p>\n<p>In <strong>CIDR notation<\/strong> (<em>Classless Inter-Domain\nRouting<\/em>), <code>/27<\/code> means that the first 27 of 32 bits of an\nIPv4 address belong to the network prefix. The remaining five bits allow\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>32<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^5 = 32<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npossible addresses. In a classical IPv4 subnet, one network address and\none broadcast address are usually reserved, leaving <strong>30\nconventional host addresses<\/strong>. This is a teaching model; special\nnetwork forms and provider allocations may differ.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-20\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Example<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Prefix bits<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Address bits for hosts<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Calculated IPv4 addresses<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>192.0.2.0/24<\/code><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">24<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">8<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">256<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>192.0.2.0/27<\/code><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">27<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">32<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>192.0.2.0/30<\/code><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">30<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Important: <strong>A subnet is not yet a complete security\nboundary.<\/strong> Effective network separation requires appropriate\nrouting and filtering rules, often supplemented by permission checks on\nendpoints and in applications. A misconfigured router can immediately\nreconnect “separate” networks.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"three-kinds-of-separation\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three kinds of separation<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Logical separation:<\/strong> for example, different virtual\nlocal area networks (<strong>VLANs<\/strong>, <em>Virtual Local Area\nNetworks<\/em>) on shared infrastructure.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Communication control:<\/strong> rules governing which\nconnections between segments are permitted, for example through\nfirewalls or access control lists.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Physical separation:<\/strong> genuinely separate cabling or\ndevices when required by the risk model; more demanding, but not\nautomatically free of errors.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The appropriate measure depends on protection requirements, threat\nmodel, operational feasibility and maintenance effort. Germany’s BSI\nexplicitly treats network architecture, segmentation and permitted\ncommunication relationships as planning topics in its IT baseline\nprotection guidance (<em>IT-Grundschutz<\/em>).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Ports, connections and firewalls: What is actually\nfiltered?<\/h2>\n<p>A network program is accessible via an address and usually an\nadditional <strong>port<\/strong>. A port is a number through which the\ntransport mechanism identifies an application endpoint. Web servers\ntypically use <code>443<\/code> for HTTPS; this is a\n<strong>convention<\/strong>, not a guarantee that a secure web service\nactually operates behind that port. Two programs can reach one another\non different ports of the same computer if the operating system and\nrules permit it.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>firewall<\/strong> examines characteristics of communication\nand decides according to rules whether traffic may pass. A\n<em>stateful<\/em> firewall typically tracks whether a packet belongs to\nan existing permitted connection. It is not equivalent to a virus\nscanner, user management system or comprehensive application security\nassessment.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Example of a comprehensible rule policy:<\/strong><\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Internet\n   |\n   | Permitted: HTTPS to public web service\n   v\n[Public web service]\n   |\n   | Permitted: defined database access\n   v\n[Internal database]\n\nInternet --------X------&gt; [Internal database]\n                     Not directly permitted<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The <strong>principle of least privilege<\/strong> can also be applied\nto networks: allow only the communication paths actually required for\nthe purpose. <strong>Default deny<\/strong> describes the basic stance of\n“deny initially, define exceptions explicitly”. Exceptions must be\ndocumented, reviewed regularly and removed when no longer needed.<\/p>\n<p>A rule as broad as “internal computers may do everything” can\nendanger an entire environment if a single computer is compromised.\nConversely, an overly complicated set of rules can encourage maintenance\nerrors: security requires not the greatest possible number of rules, but\nverifiable, correct rules.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Security objectives and threat model<\/h2>\n<p>Three classical <strong>security objectives<\/strong> of information\nsecurity are:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Confidentiality:<\/strong> Unauthorised people should not be\nable to read data.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Integrity:<\/strong> Data and functions should not be changed\nunnoticed or without authorisation.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Availability:<\/strong> Authorised people should be able to\nuse required services reliably.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Other objectives include <strong>authenticity<\/strong> (does a\nmessage actually originate from the expected sender?) and\n<strong>traceability<\/strong> (can security-relevant operations be\nadequately reconstructed?). Security objectives may conflict. More\nmonitoring might detect an attack earlier, but at the same time collect\nunnecessary personal data. A highly isolated service may be more\ndifficult to administer.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"the-threat-model-in-five-questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The threat model in five\nquestions<\/h3>\n<p>Before choosing products and protection measures, one should ask:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>What should be protected?<\/strong> For example, private\nmedia, customer data, access keys or website availability.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Who or what can cause harm?<\/strong> Accidental misuse,\ndefective hardware, malware, compromised accounts, abusive use or an\nunreliable service provider.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Which boundaries are crossed?<\/strong> Browser to server,\nserver to database, office PC to IoT device, own server to cloud\nservice.<\/li>\n<li><strong>What damage is realistic?<\/strong> Data loss, disclosure,\nservice interruption, incorrect invoices, uncontrolled usage costs.<\/li>\n<li><strong>How would one notice a disruption and respond?<\/strong>\nLogs, alerts, account suspension, recovery and communication.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>A <strong>risk<\/strong> does not depend solely on a theoretical\nattack possibility. It takes account of the plausibility of an incident\nand its possible scale. Numerical models can support prioritisation but\nare uncertain for rare security events.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Deliberately fictional example:<\/strong> A service might\nincur approximately 2,000 euros in additional processing costs over 100\nhours of downtime. This amounts to <code>20 euros per hour<\/code> as a\nmodel calculation, not an actual price or a reliable estimate of loss.\nEven without an attacker, a faulty backup can cause comparable damage.\nThe threat model should therefore include <strong>accidents and\nfailures<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Encryption: What HTTPS does and does not achieve<\/h2>\n<p><strong>HTTPS<\/strong> combines HTTP with a protected transport\nchannel, today typically using <strong>TLS<\/strong> (<em>Transport Layer\nSecurity<\/em>). TLS is intended to protect confidentiality and integrity\nduring transmission and appropriately authenticate the other endpoint. A\nbrowser typically checks a certificate for the expected domain name and\na trusted certificate chain. <strong>TLS does not protect against a\nmalicious application on the correctly authenticated server<\/strong>,\nagainst abuse by authorised logged-in users, or against all metadata\nobservation.<\/p>\n<p>Since July 2026, <strong>RFC 9846<\/strong> has described TLS 1.3 and\nsuperseded the earlier TLS 1.3 specification RFC 8446. This does not\nmean that the protocol suddenly acquired the version number “TLS 1.4”:\nit remains <strong>TLS 1.3<\/strong>, but the relevant RFC document has\nbeen revised. For configuration recommendations, actual browser, server\nand library support is also relevant.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser                          HTTPS server\n   |                                  |\n   | Connection setup, negotiation    |\n   |---------------------------------&gt;|\n   |&lt;---------------------------------|\n   | Certificate / peer verification  |\n   |                                  |\n   | &lt; Protected data exchange &gt;      |\n   |==================================|<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Transport security<\/strong> exists only between the\nrespective TLS endpoints. If a reverse proxy terminates the TLS\nconnection and subsequently forwards traffic internally without\nencryption, only the first part of the route is encrypted. A second TLS\nconnection can be useful, but it does not replace access control.\n<strong>End-to-end encryption<\/strong> is a separate concept: only the\nintended endpoints have access to the decrypted content; ordinary HTTPS\nconnections to a website do not automatically constitute such an\narchitecture.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"do-not-confuse-hashes-signatures-and-passwords\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Do not confuse\nhashes, signatures and passwords<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>cryptographic hash function<\/strong> computes a compact\nverification value from data. It is not encryption: it should not be\nstraightforward to reconstruct the original text from the hash.<\/li>\n<li>A <strong>digital signature<\/strong> helps to verify origin and\nintegrity when the associated keys are managed correctly.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Password hashes<\/strong> require special slow, salted\nmethods for human-chosen passwords; a fast general-purpose checksum hash\nalone is unsuitable.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Encryption<\/strong> protects content only if keys are\nprotected, access permissions are correct and plaintext copies are\nhandled appropriately.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The most common error is to regard cryptography as a universal\nsolution. An encrypted storage device is of little help if the\napplication exposes all records to every user after login.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Login, permissions and sessions: Three different\nquestions<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Authentication<\/strong> answers “Who is logging in?”, whereas\n<strong>authorisation<\/strong> answers “What may this authenticated\nactor do?”. A successful login does not automatically mean that every\nrecord may be read. A <strong>session<\/strong> associates multiple\nrequests with an already established login state. During its validity\nperiod, a session token can have a similar effect to the original\nauthentication credential and must be protected accordingly.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)<\/strong> uses evidence from\ndifferent factor types, such as knowledge, possession and biometric\ncharacteristics. Additional factors mitigate many risks, but not all\nmethods are equally robust against phishing. <strong>Passkeys<\/strong>,\nbased on FIDO2/WebAuthn, can enable phishing-resistant authentication\nbound to the correct service. Device loss, account recovery and\nincorrect permission checks remain important issues even with this\napproach.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Example<\/th>\n<th>Question<\/th>\n<th>Appropriate control point<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Successful login<\/td>\n<td>Is it the correct person or key?<\/td>\n<td>Authentication<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Retrieve media catalogue <code>4711<\/code><\/td>\n<td>Does the entry belong to this user?<\/td>\n<td>Server-side authorisation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cookies after login<\/td>\n<td>Was an unpredictable, correctly protected token issued?<\/td>\n<td>Session management<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Access expired<\/td>\n<td>May old requests still be accepted?<\/td>\n<td>Expiry and revocation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>Important architectural principle:<\/strong> A hidden button\nin a user interface is not a permissions model. The application must\ncheck access on the server or a comparably trusted component <strong>for\nevery relevant operation<\/strong>. Even if an attacker guesses an\naddress or manually constructs a request, other users’ data must not be\ndisclosed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Typical application weaknesses: The OWASP perspective<\/h2>\n<p>The <strong>Open Worldwide Application Security Project\n(OWASP)<\/strong> community publishes security material for web\napplications. The <strong>OWASP Top 10:2025<\/strong> is an awareness\nlist of major risk categories, <strong>not a comprehensive security\ncatalogue or a guarantee of particular frequencies in one’s own\nproject<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Area (OWASP 2025)<\/th>\n<th>What can go wrong?<\/th>\n<th>Defence principle<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A01 Broken Access Control<\/td>\n<td>Users access other users’ data or functions.<\/td>\n<td>Server-side checks of each object and operation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A02 Security Misconfiguration<\/td>\n<td>Debug mode, unnecessary services, overly permissive rules.<\/td>\n<td>Secure initial configuration, traceable changes<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A03 Software Supply Chain Failures<\/td>\n<td>Dependencies, builds or distribution paths are compromised.<\/td>\n<td>Verify provenance, updates, permissions and approvals<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A04 Cryptographic Failures<\/td>\n<td>Data is protected incorrectly or incompletely.<\/td>\n<td>Established standards, correct key and certificate management<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A05 Injection<\/td>\n<td>External input is inadvertently treated as a command.<\/td>\n<td>Parameterised queries and appropriate interfaces<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A06 Insecure Design<\/td>\n<td>Protection rules are already missing from the basic model.<\/td>\n<td>Threat modelling, data flow and permission design<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A07 Authentication Failures<\/td>\n<td>Login, account recovery or tokens are insecure.<\/td>\n<td>Appropriate factors, secure sessions and revocation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A08 Software or Data Integrity Failures<\/td>\n<td>Untrusted changes are accepted.<\/td>\n<td>Integrity checks and controlled update paths<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A09 Logging and Alerting Failures<\/td>\n<td>Incidents remain undetected or cannot be reconstructed.<\/td>\n<td>Purposeful, data-minimising event logs<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A10 Mishandling of Exceptional Conditions<\/td>\n<td>Exceptional states cause unexpected, insecure behaviour.<\/td>\n<td>Defined error paths and secure default responses<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>SQL injection<\/strong> arises, for example, when uncontrolled\ncharacters from a form are interpreted as part of an SQL command.\n<strong>Parameterised queries<\/strong> separate data values from\ncommands. <strong>Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)<\/strong> concerns content\nprocessed as active code in the wrong browser context;\ncontext-appropriate output encoding is central here. <strong>Server-Side\nRequest Forgery (SSRF)<\/strong> describes the misuse of server-side\naccess to unwanted destinations; network segmentation alone cannot\neliminate this flaw completely.<\/p>\n<p>OWASP changed its categories in 2025 compared with 2021: for example,\nA03 now covers software supply chain failures, while misconfigurations\nare under A02. Anyone comparing an older list with a newer one must\ntherefore pay attention to <strong>the year and category names<\/strong>,\notherwise different issues are incorrectly equated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Operational security: Patching, monitoring and recovery<\/h2>\n<p>A secure architectural diagram does not guarantee a secure service\nafter a year of operation. Software receives updates, certificates\nexpire, devices are replaced, permissions remain after role changes and\nexternal dependencies change. Security is therefore an\n<strong>operational process<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"the-lifecycle-model\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The lifecycle model<\/h3>\n<p>The <strong>NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0<\/strong> describes six\ninterconnected functions: <strong>Govern<\/strong>,\n<strong>Identify<\/strong>, <strong>Protect<\/strong>,\n<strong>Detect<\/strong>, <strong>Respond<\/strong> and\n<strong>Recover<\/strong>. These phases are not simply a staircase to be\nclimbed once; many activities run continuously in parallel.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Govern / Responsibilities and objectives\n            |\nIdentify -&gt; Protect -&gt; Detect -&gt; Respond -&gt; Recover\n    ^                                 |         |\n    +-------- Lessons learned &lt;-------+---------+<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>NIST SP 800-61 Revision 3<\/strong> of April 2025 explicitly\nplaces incident handling within this risk management framework. It\nreplaces the older Revision 2. <strong>Incident response<\/strong> means\nknowing responsibilities, appropriately recording an incident, limiting\ndamage, addressing causes, recovering service and feeding lessons back\ninto operations. In addition to technology, this encompasses\norganisational decisions and potentially legal obligations.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"what-a-small-service-actually-needs\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What a small service\nactually needs<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inventory:<\/strong> Which servers, accounts, software\nversions, domains, databases and service providers exist in the first\nplace?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Planned updates:<\/strong> Track security-relevant updates\nand deploy them with a suitable fallback option; do not blindly put\nevery version into production without testing.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Key and account maintenance:<\/strong> Disable unnecessary\naccounts, keep secrets out of source code, minimise access permissions\nand review them after changes.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Limited logging:<\/strong> Trace failed logins, unusual\npermission-related access and failures; do not write passwords, session\ntokens or unnecessary personal content to logs.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Recovery:<\/strong> Create backups <strong>and<\/strong>\nverify recovery regularly. A backup that has never been restored is not\nyet a proven recovery strategy.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Operational limits:<\/strong> Rate limits, capacity planning,\nalert thresholds and budget controls can limit abuse and unintended\ncosts.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The mere existence of a firewall does not fully address any of these\ntopics. Conversely, a single small hobby program does not need to\nimitate a full-time security team. What matters is a small number of\nmeasures that can genuinely be operated and understood in the particular\ncontext.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Virtual private networks, zero trust and remote access<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>VPN<\/strong> (<em>Virtual Private Network<\/em>) establishes\na logically protected communication path over another network. This can\nbe useful if employees or one’s own devices are to access internal\nservices. However, a VPN does not prevent an <em>already authorised and\ncompromised device<\/em> from abusing internal services. It replaces\nneither permission checks nor updated endpoints.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Zero trust<\/strong> is not a single product and does not mean\n“nobody is allowed to do anything”. The fundamental idea is that network\nposition alone does not confer lasting trust. Access is justified\naccording to identity, device state, resource, context and policy, and\nlimited as narrowly as possible. There are gradual transitions: a small\nprivate system can implement the most important idea with separate user\naccounts and the fewest possible privileges without building a complex\nenterprise platform.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Model<\/th>\n<th>Strength<\/th>\n<th>Limitation<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Home router/NAT only<\/td>\n<td>Simple default shielding against many incoming contacts<\/td>\n<td>Not a complete protection and permissions architecture<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Segmentation and firewall<\/td>\n<td>Limits communication paths between areas<\/td>\n<td>Does not protect against every application weakness<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>VPN<\/td>\n<td>Protected remote access to selected networks<\/td>\n<td>Scope of trust may be too broad<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Identity- and resource-based checks<\/td>\n<td>Narrower access to specific services<\/td>\n<td>Requires maintained identities and rules<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Completely separate infrastructure<\/td>\n<td>Reduces some shared failure and attack paths<\/td>\n<td>More devices, costs and operational effort<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>When is it excessive?<\/strong> A purely offline media\ncatalogue that exposes no data externally needs no VPN access, public\nidentity provider or internet firewall in front of a non-existent web\nAPI. <strong>When is it necessary?<\/strong> As soon as sensitive data\nbelonging to other people or administrative access becomes publicly\naccessible, protection requirements and the consequences of an error\nincrease considerably.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three continuous application architectures<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"example-a-the-local-media-catalogue\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example A – The local media\ncatalogue<\/h3>\n<p>A program runs only on a personal computer and manages local video\nand image metadata. The main danger here may be a faulty update or loss\nof the hard drive, rather than a targeted attack on a public API.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Local computer\n  |-- User interface\n  |-- Local application\n  |-- SQLite / files\n  \\-- Separate backup copy\n\nNo publicly accessible web service required<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Useful:<\/strong> Keep the operating system up to date, use\nstandard user privileges, test backups and consciously select downloaded\nprograms and dependencies. <strong>Not automatically useful:<\/strong>\none’s own cloud server, external user management, microservices and\ncomplex network traffic. If synchronisation is introduced later, new\ntrust boundaries arise – the model must then be reassessed.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"example-b-a-public-web-application-with-a-database\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example B –\nA public web application with a database<\/h3>\n<p>A website provides private users with an analysis function. The\nfrontend may be public, but the database must not be directly accessible\nfrom the internet. The web service checks login and the record’s\nrelationship to the user.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser / internet\n       |\n      HTTPS\n       |\n       v\nPublic web service / reverse proxy\n       |\n       v\nAPI: Authentication + object authorisation + limits\n       |\n       | Defined internal connection only\n       v\nPrivate database\n       |\n       v\nTested backups / recovery<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Typical errors:<\/strong> The database is accidentally given\npublic network access, a new API route omits the object check, a backup\nis placed in the web directory or logs contain confidential tokens.\n<strong>Pragmatic countermeasures:<\/strong> Only necessary connections,\nserver-side authorisation, secure software configuration, an update\npath, access logs and tested recovery.<\/p>\n<p>Cost is part of the question: a second instance, an external firewall\nservice or a continuous security scanner costs money and maintenance\neffort. These are not automatically the most sensible first investment;\nsystematic permission checks and a working backup can provide greater\nimmediate benefit.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"example-c-a-connected-sensor-with-cloud-integration\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example C –\nA connected sensor with cloud integration<\/h3>\n<p>A microcontroller sends measurements to a central service. The device\nmay be used for years and may have limited memory and processing power.\nThe loss of a single device key or a faulty update can already affect\nnumerous devices.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Sensor -&gt; Microcontroller -&gt; Secured transmission path\n                                  |\n                                  v\n                          Device ingress / API\n                                  |\n                           Separate device identities\n                                  |\n                                  v\n                             Storage / analysis<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Security objectives:<\/strong> Measurements should not be\narbitrarily manipulable, an individual device must not automatically be\nable to administer other devices, and necessary updates must be\ndeployable in a controlled manner. <strong>Limitations:<\/strong>\nEncryption requires energy and computing time; certificate and key\nrotation must work despite intermittent connectivity. If the sensor\nmerely controls a local lamp, a mandatory cloud connection would instead\nbe an additional source of failure without a compelling benefit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two small calculations: Size is not the same as\nsecurity<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"a-27-subnet\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A /27 subnet<\/h3>\n<p>For IPv4 with 32 bits and <code>/27<\/code>, 5 bits are not reserved\nfor the network prefix:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>32<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>27<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">32 - 27 = 5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe result is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>32<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^5 = 32<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\naddresses in the block. In the classical subnet with network and\nbroadcast addresses, 30 host addresses remain. The calculation explains\n<strong>address capacity<\/strong>, not how many of these addresses can\nactually be operated securely. It also says nothing about which\nconnections the firewall permits.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"why-bandwidth-and-latency-are-different\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why bandwidth and\nlatency are different<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Hypothetical:<\/strong> A 100 MB file contains approximately\n800 megabits in a simplified calculation. At an ideal 100 Mbit/s,\ntransmission alone takes at least\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>800<\/mn><mn>100<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">seconds<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{800}{100}=8\\,\\text{seconds}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nProtocol overhead, competing use, server load and packet losses increase\nthe actual time. A 10 ms latency to a server does not make bandwidth\narbitrarily large; a 1 Gbit/s connection does not automatically make a\nslow database query fast.<\/p>\n<p>Security also affects the architecture: additional checks create\nwork, but omitting fundamental access controls is not a permissible\noptimisation. One should measure <em>which<\/em> check incurs costs\nbefore reducing the protection architecture.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Decision framework and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Five short questions help before implementing a technical measure:\n<strong>What problem is being solved? Who is at risk? What is the\nsimplest effective protection? Who maintains it? How will its failure be\ndetected?<\/strong><\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation<\/th>\n<th>Often useful<\/th>\n<th>Often unnecessary or insufficient<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Single offline PC<\/td>\n<td>Updates, permissions, backup, trustworthy software<\/td>\n<td>Public API and enterprise zero trust platform<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Home network with guest devices<\/td>\n<td>Separation, router maintenance, separate access<\/td>\n<td>Blind trust based on the same Wi-Fi SSID<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Small public web service<\/td>\n<td>HTTPS, server permissions, patch maintenance, logs, restore\ntest<\/td>\n<td>Merely “port 443 is open, so everything is secure”<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cloud with sensitive data<\/td>\n<td>Protection requirements, minimised IAM permissions, separate\nresources<\/td>\n<td>Unquestioned trust in the cloud provider<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Long-lived IoT device<\/td>\n<td>Device identities, update path, protection against key loss<\/td>\n<td>Mandatory internet connectivity for every function<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The most important relationship is this: <strong>Networks transport\ndata. Security determines the conditions under which that transport and\nsubsequent processing are permitted.<\/strong> Secure communication,\nsecure applications, secure data storage and secure operation are\ndifferent tasks that must fit together.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guide to terminology<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term<\/th>\n<th>Brief description<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>IP<\/td>\n<td>Addressing and forwarding between networks<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TCP / UDP / QUIC<\/td>\n<td>Different transport mechanisms for application data<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DNS<\/td>\n<td>Name resolution; not a substitute for website authentication<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Port<\/td>\n<td>Technical endpoint of network communication<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CIDR<\/td>\n<td>Description of network prefixes, e.g. <code>/27<\/code><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>VLAN<\/td>\n<td>Logical local network segment<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Firewall<\/td>\n<td>Rule-based control of communication flows<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TLS<\/td>\n<td>Protection of data transmission and peer verification<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MFA / Passkey<\/td>\n<td>Additional or phishing-resistant authentication respectively<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Authorisation<\/td>\n<td>Verification of which operation an actor may perform<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Least privilege<\/td>\n<td>Grant only necessary permissions<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Zero trust<\/td>\n<td>Access based on verified contexts, not merely network location<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Incident response<\/td>\n<td>Orderly handling of a security incident<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Restore test<\/td>\n<td>Practical demonstration that a backup can be restored<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Position within the course-wide reading paths<\/h2>\n<p>This is an <strong>optional Bachelor’s elective lesson<\/strong>, not\nan additional main-course lesson. The conceptual bridge leads, among\nother places, to <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L4 in the\nMaster’s core<\/a>. Further routes: <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;page=path&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">course-wide reading paths<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;page=index&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">course index<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source review:<\/strong> 04.11.2026<\/p>\n<p><strong>Date of online source review:<\/strong> 04.11.2026. The\nfollowing links lead to official standards bodies and specialist\nprojects. They substantiate definitions, architectural principles and\nrecommendations; they are not certification or security approval for a\nparticular system.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>NIST<\/strong>, <em>Cybersecurity Framework (CSF) 2.0<\/em>,\nwith six functions including GOVERN: <a\nhref=\"https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework\">https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework/faqs\">https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework/faqs<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>NIST<\/strong>, <em>SP 800-61 Revision 3<\/em>, finalised\nApril 2025, incident handling within the CSF 2.0 framework: <a\nhref=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/61/r3/final\">https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/61/r3/final<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>OWASP<\/strong>, <em>Top 10:2025<\/em>, current risk\ncategories for web applications: <a\nhref=\"https://top10.owasp.org/2025/\">https://top10.owasp.org/2025/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\nor the German overview <a\nhref=\"https://top10.owasp.org/2025/de/\">https://top10.owasp.org/2025/de/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>OWASP Cheat Sheet Series<\/strong>, <em>Authorization<\/em>,\n<em>Session Management<\/em> and <em>Input Validation<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html\">https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n, <a\nhref=\"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Session_Management_Cheat_Sheet\">https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Session_Management_Cheat_Sheet<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n, <a\nhref=\"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Input_Validation_Cheat_Sheet.html\">https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Input_Validation_Cheat_Sheet.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>IETF / RFC Editor<\/strong>, <em>RFC 9846 – The Transport\nLayer Security (TLS) Protocol Version 1.3<\/em>, July 2026; supersedes\nRFC 8446: <a\nhref=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc9846/\">https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc9846/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>IETF / RFC Editor<\/strong>, <em>RFC 8200 – Internet\nProtocol, Version 6 (IPv6) Specification<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc8200/\">https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc8200/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>BSI<\/strong>, <em>IT-Grundschutz, NET.1.1 Netzarchitektur\nund -design<\/em>; segmentation and controlled communication\nrelationships as planning topics. Original document (2023 edition): <a\nhref=\"https://www.bsi.bund.de/SharedDocs/Downloads/DE/BSI/Grundschutz/IT-GS-Kompendium_Einzel_PDFs_2023/09_NET_Netze_und_Kommunikation/NET_1_1_Netzarchitektur_und_design_Edition_2023.pdf?__blob=publicationFile&amp;v=3\">https://www.bsi.bund.de/SharedDocs/Downloads/DE/BSI/Grundschutz/IT-GS-Kompendium_Einzel_PDFs_2023/09_NET_Netze_und_Kommunikation/NET_1_1_Netzarchitektur_und_design_Edition_2023.pdf?__blob=publicationFile&amp;v=3<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further course connections:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L6 – Networks and security<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L5 – Cloud networks and\nscaling<\/a>, <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L3 – Security, data\nprotection and responsibility<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L1 – Discrete computer science<\/a>,\n<a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L3 – Embedded systems<\/a>.\n<strong>Next possible Bachelor’s elective lesson:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L2 – Advanced data modelling and\ndatabase queries<\/a>. Master’s elective topics and the later Master’s\nspecialisation F remain planned independently of this.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M10-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M10-L2 – Data modelling and database queries in depth<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Bachelor’s: Networks and databases<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L5<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L1<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L5<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L1<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Classification:<\/strong> Understanding computer science –\noptional Bachelor elective topic 8 of 8.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Note:<\/strong> The aim is to reason through a real\ninformation task so that it becomes clear which tables are useful, what\nqueries actually evaluate, why apparently identical queries return\ndifferent results and what operational consequences a database structure\nentails.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Data modelling: the actual question comes before the\ndatabase<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Data modelling<\/strong> means translating real objects,\nprocesses and relationships into a comprehensible structure of data and\nrules. It begins neither with the choice of a database product nor with\nwriting SQL. First, we need to answer: <em>Which things exist in the\ndomain, how do we recognise them again, and which statements about them\nmust remain true over time?<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Imagine a local media catalogue. It manages channels, videos and\ntags. In this simplified example, each video belongs to exactly one\nchannel; a video may have several tags, and a tag may be assigned to\nseveral videos. A channel may exist before its first video has been\nrecorded. A video may initially have no tags.<\/p>\n<p>These seemingly unremarkable sentences are already important\n<strong>business rules<\/strong> – more precisely, domain consistency\nrules. They determine the subsequent tables, keys and the nature of the\nsearch queries. Here, ‘business’ does not imply making a profit;\nsoftware engineering also uses the term for the rules of a private\narchive.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Real question: Which videos of a channel have the tag &#39;Natur&#39; (nature)?\n                   |\n                   v\nDomain model: Channel -- owns --&gt; Video &lt;-- has --&gt; Tag\n                   |\n                   v\nLogical model: Tables, unique keys and relationships\n                   |\n                   v\nPhysical implementation: specific DBMS, indexes, storage, backups\n                   |\n                   v\nSQL query --&gt; filtered, ordered result rows<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Three levels should be distinguished:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Level<\/th>\n<th>Guiding question<\/th>\n<th>Example<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Conceptual<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>What exists in the domain?<\/td>\n<td>Channels, videos and tags; a channel has many videos<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Logical<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>How is the information structurally related?<\/td>\n<td><code>kanal_id<\/code> as a reference from <code>video<\/code> to\n<code>kanal<\/code><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Physical<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>How is the design implemented in the chosen system?<\/td>\n<td>Data types, B-tree indexes, storage layout, partitioning<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>Use:<\/strong> Even for a small catalogue used over a longer\nperiod, this approach helps avoid ambiguities later.\n<strong>Overengineering:<\/strong> A formal diagram is usually\nunnecessary for five unchanging values in a configuration file.\n<strong>Risk:<\/strong> Oversimplifying reality leads to later data loss\nor contradictory special cases – for example, if a video can belong to\nseveral channels, contrary to the original assumption.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Entities, properties, keys and cardinalities<\/h2>\n<p>An <strong>entity<\/strong> is a distinct object in the domain, such\nas a video. Its <strong>attributes<\/strong> are properties, such as\ntitle, publication date and number of views. A\n<strong>relationship<\/strong> describes a verifiable connection, such as\n‘video belongs to channel’.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>cardinality<\/strong> describes how many objects may be\nlinked to one another. Common examples are:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>1:1:<\/strong> A person has at most one particular profile,\nprovided that the domain model explicitly stipulates this rule.<\/li>\n<li><strong>1:n:<\/strong> A channel owns many videos; each video refers\nto one channel.<\/li>\n<li><strong>n:m:<\/strong> Videos and tags may each be associated with\nseveral of the other kind; this usually requires a junction table.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Optionality<\/strong> must also be clarified: must a\nrelationship exist? In our example, a video has exactly one channel but\npossibly zero tags. There is a crucial difference between\n<code>NULL<\/code> meaning ‘no channel known’ and a video without a\nchannel that is prohibited by the domain rules.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>KANAL (1) ---------------- (0..n) VIDEO\n                                 |\n                                 | (0..n)\n                                 |\n                            VIDEO_TAG\n                                 |\n                                 | (0..n)\n                                 v\n                               TAG\n\nVIDEO_TAG connects the two sides of the n:m relationship.<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A <strong>primary key<\/strong> uniquely identifies a table row; a\n<strong>foreign key<\/strong> enforces valid references. A\n<strong>composite identifier<\/strong> may consist of several columns: in\n<code>video_tag<\/code>, the pair <code>(video_id, tag_id)<\/code> makes a\nsuitable primary key. This prevents the same tag from being assigned\ntwice in exactly the same way to the same video.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>natural key<\/strong> derives from domain data, such as a\ngenuinely immutable external identifier. A <strong>surrogate\nkey<\/strong> is an ID specifically assigned for this purpose. Both have\nadvantages and disadvantages: names can change and are often not\nglobally unique; internal IDs must be maintained and correctly matched\nduring import. An external video ID can be protected separately with\n<code>UNIQUE<\/code>, without automatically making it the internal\nprimary key.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Time is also part of the model.<\/strong> ‘Published on’,\n‘last imported’ and ‘number of views at the time of measurement’ are\ndifferent statements. If a view count is recorded daily, a single column\n<code>aufrufe<\/code> is no longer sufficient to represent its history;\nan additional measurement table with timestamps would then be\nuseful.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A small relational model for reading<\/h2>\n<p>The following schema is deliberately reduced in scope. SQL stands for\n<em>Structured Query Language<\/em>. The <code>CREATE TABLE<\/code>\nstatements do not describe an assignment to be carried out, but rules\nthat a database could subsequently enforce.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb3\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb3-1\"><a href=\"#cb3-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">CREATE<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">TABLE<\/span> kanal (<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-2\"><a href=\"#cb3-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    kanal_id <span class=\"dt\">INTEGER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">PRIMARY<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">KEY<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-3\"><a href=\"#cb3-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    name <span class=\"dt\">VARCHAR<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">120<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">NOT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NULL<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">UNIQUE<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-4\"><a href=\"#cb3-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>);<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-5\"><a href=\"#cb3-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-6\"><a href=\"#cb3-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">CREATE<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">TABLE<\/span> video (<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-7\"><a href=\"#cb3-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    video_id <span class=\"dt\">INTEGER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">PRIMARY<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">KEY<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-8\"><a href=\"#cb3-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    kanal_id <span class=\"dt\">INTEGER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NOT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NULL<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">REFERENCES<\/span> kanal(kanal_id)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-9\"><a href=\"#cb3-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"kw\">ON<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">DELETE<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">RESTRICT<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-10\"><a href=\"#cb3-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    titel <span class=\"dt\">VARCHAR<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">200<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">NOT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NULL<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-11\"><a href=\"#cb3-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    veroeffentlicht_am <span class=\"dt\">DATE<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NOT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NULL<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-12\"><a href=\"#cb3-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    aufrufe <span class=\"dt\">INTEGER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NOT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NULL<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">DEFAULT<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">CHECK<\/span> (aufrufe <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-13\"><a href=\"#cb3-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>);<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-14\"><a href=\"#cb3-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-15\"><a href=\"#cb3-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">CREATE<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">TABLE<\/span> tag (<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-16\"><a href=\"#cb3-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    tag_id <span class=\"dt\">INTEGER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">PRIMARY<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">KEY<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-17\"><a href=\"#cb3-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    name <span class=\"dt\">VARCHAR<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">80<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">NOT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NULL<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">UNIQUE<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-18\"><a href=\"#cb3-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>);<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-19\"><a href=\"#cb3-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-20\"><a href=\"#cb3-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">CREATE<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">TABLE<\/span> video_tag (<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-21\"><a href=\"#cb3-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    video_id <span class=\"dt\">INTEGER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NOT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NULL<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">REFERENCES<\/span> video(video_id)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-22\"><a href=\"#cb3-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"kw\">ON<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">DELETE<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">CASCADE<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-23\"><a href=\"#cb3-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    tag_id <span class=\"dt\">INTEGER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NOT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NULL<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">REFERENCES<\/span> tag(tag_id)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-24\"><a href=\"#cb3-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"kw\">ON<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">DELETE<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">RESTRICT<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-25\"><a href=\"#cb3-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"kw\">PRIMARY<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">KEY<\/span> (video_id, tag_id)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-26\"><a href=\"#cb3-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>);<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p><strong><code>NOT NULL<\/code><\/strong> requires a value to be\npresent. <strong><code>UNIQUE<\/code><\/strong> limits duplicates; when\n<code>NULL<\/code> values are permitted, product-specific rules need to\nbe considered. <strong><code>CHECK<\/code><\/strong> tests an expression\nsuch as ‘the number of views is not negative’.\n<strong><code>REFERENCES<\/code><\/strong> specifies the foreign key.\n<strong><code>ON DELETE RESTRICT<\/code><\/strong> is intended to prevent\nthe deletion of a referenced parent entry, whereas\n<strong><code>ON DELETE CASCADE<\/code><\/strong> also deletes the\nassociated junction rows. These deletion rules are domain decisions: a\ncareless cascade can remove far more data than expected.<\/p>\n<p>The syntax of this example is largely readable by both SQLite and\nPostgreSQL, but <strong>not identical in every detail<\/strong>. SQLite\nuses flexible typing, so <code>DATE<\/code> does not automatically\nguarantee the same behaviour as PostgreSQL’s <code>DATE<\/code> data\ntype. Automatic ID assignment also differs across systems unless an\nexplicit mechanism is defined. In addition, SQLite requires foreign-key\nenforcement to be deliberately enabled or checked for each connection;\nits documented default is <code>OFF<\/code> for compatibility reasons,\nunless compiled otherwise. A declared foreign-key relationship is\ntherefore no proof that it is actually enforced in every application\nruntime.<\/p>\n<p>To trace the queries by hand, we use these <strong>fictional initial\ndata<\/strong> (the literal values and identifiers in the SQL examples\nare retained):<\/p>\n<p><strong><code>kanal<\/code><\/strong><\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">kanal_id<\/th>\n<th>name<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td>Wissen<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td>Technik<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3<\/td>\n<td>Leerkanal<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong><code>video<\/code><\/strong><\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">video_id<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">kanal_id<\/th>\n<th>titel<\/th>\n<th>veroeffentlicht_am<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">aufrufe<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">101<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td>Bäume<\/td>\n<td>2026-01-10<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">120<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">102<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td>Flüsse<\/td>\n<td>2026-02-01<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">80<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">201<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td>Netze<\/td>\n<td>2026-02-03<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">200<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong><code>tag<\/code> and <code>video_tag<\/code><\/strong><\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">tag_id<\/th>\n<th>Name<\/th>\n<th>Associated videos<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">7<\/td>\n<td>Natur<\/td>\n<td>101, 102<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">8<\/td>\n<td>Technik<\/td>\n<td>201<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The third channel row, which has no video, is not an error; it will\nlater reveal a difference between an <strong>INNER JOIN<\/strong> and a\n<strong>LEFT JOIN<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why tables are separated: functional dependencies and\nnormalisation<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>functional dependency<\/strong> states that when a certain\nkey value is fixed, the value of another attribute is thereby\ndetermined. In the simplified model, for example,\n<code>kanal_id -&gt; kanal.name<\/code> holds. The channel name therefore\nlogically belongs to the channel, not to each video.<\/p>\n<p>An unfavourable single table might look like this:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>video_id | videotitel | kanal_id | kanalname | tag\n101      | Bäume      | 1        | Wissen    | Natur\n102      | Flüsse     | 1        | Wissen    | Natur\n201      | Netze      | 2        | Technik   | Technik<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>If channel 1 receives a new name, the same value must be changed in\nseveral places. If a row is forgotten, an <strong>update\nanomaly<\/strong> arises. Deleting the only video of a channel might\naccidentally remove the information about the channel itself\n(<strong>deletion anomaly<\/strong>). It may be impossible to record a\nnew channel without a video (<strong>insertion anomaly<\/strong>).<\/p>\n<p><strong>Normalisation<\/strong> is a systematic way of organising such\nrepetitions and dependencies. For reading purposes, it is sufficient to\ndevelop an accurate basic intuition for three frequently discussed\nnormal forms:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Normal form<\/th>\n<th>In simplified terms<\/th>\n<th>What question do we ask?<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>1NF<\/strong> (first normal form)<\/td>\n<td>Values are atomic within the chosen relational model; no repeating\ncolumn groups<\/td>\n<td>Are ‘tag1, tag2, tag3’ placed next to each other as artificially\nfixed columns?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>2NF<\/strong> (second normal form)<\/td>\n<td>In 1NF; non-key attributes do not depend on just a proper subset of\na composite candidate key<\/td>\n<td>Does a tag name really belong to the assignment\n<code>(video_id, tag_id)<\/code> or to the table <code>tag<\/code>?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>3NF<\/strong> (third normal form)<\/td>\n<td>In 2NF; impermissible transitive non-key dependencies are\navoided<\/td>\n<td>Does <code>video_id<\/code> determine <code>kanal_id<\/code>, which in\nturn determines the channel name?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The formal definitions are more precise than these rules of thumb and\nrequire clarity about <strong>candidate keys<\/strong> and the relevant\nfunctional dependencies, among other things. An artificially unique ID\nkey alone does not eliminate domain redundancy.<\/p>\n<p>By separating <code>kanal<\/code>, <code>video<\/code>,\n<code>tag<\/code> and <code>video_tag<\/code>, the respective name is\nmaintained at the appropriate place in the domain model.\n<strong>Denormalisation<\/strong> can nevertheless be useful: a very\nfrequently queried counter or a precomputed summary is sometimes stored\nin addition. Updating, staleness and consistency then become new\nproblems. A fast report is not automatically a better data model.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is normalisation excessive?<\/strong> In a small, one-off\nexported analysis, repeating column values may be readable and\npractical. In a database that is continually edited, the rules are more\nimportant. Image files, videos and free text do not necessarily have to\nbe broken down into relational tables in every detail either.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>SQL queries are declarative: the result is described<\/h2>\n<p>An SQL query primarily describes <strong>which rows and\ncolumns<\/strong> are needed. The database optimiser may choose the\nexecution path itself. In simplified terms, the following conceptual\nstages can be distinguished:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>FROM / JOIN  -&gt;  WHERE  -&gt;  GROUP BY  -&gt;  HAVING\n     -&gt; SELECT expressions  -&gt;  ORDER BY  -&gt;  LIMIT<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This is a <strong>didactic logical model<\/strong>, not a binding\nphysical execution order. Window functions and database-specific details\nin particular need closer consideration. The optimiser can apply filters\nearly, arrange joins differently or use indexes as long as the semantics\nare preserved.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb6\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb6-1\"><a href=\"#cb6-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">SELECT<\/span> titel, aufrufe<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb6-2\"><a href=\"#cb6-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">FROM<\/span> video<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb6-3\"><a href=\"#cb6-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">WHERE<\/span> kanal_id <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">AND<\/span> aufrufe <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">100<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb6-4\"><a href=\"#cb6-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">ORDER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">BY<\/span> aufrufe <span class=\"kw\">DESC<\/span>, video_id <span class=\"kw\">ASC<\/span>;<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Of our three video rows, the query returns only ‘Bäume’ with 120\nviews. <code>WHERE<\/code> excludes rows that do not meet the criterion.\n<code>ORDER BY<\/code> specifies the order explicitly; without this\nclause, a stable order is <strong>not guaranteed<\/strong>, even if\nindividual tests always produce the same picture.<\/p>\n<p><strong>SQL generally uses multisets rather than pure mathematical\nsets.<\/strong> A result table may contain several identical-looking\nrows. <code>DISTINCT<\/code> removes duplicate results with respect to\nthe selected columns, but not the underlying records. This is especially\nimportant for joins and aggregates.<\/p>\n<p><strong><code>LIMIT<\/code><\/strong> restricts the output but is not a\nsubstitute for a sort order defined by the domain. If an interface is\nintended to display ‘the 20 newest videos’, it should use a unique\nsecondary sort key in addition to the timestamp; simultaneous\npublications would otherwise not have a unique order. The exact syntax\nfor limiting results varies between database products.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>JOIN: reading relationships without inadvertently\nmultiplying data<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>JOIN<\/strong> combines rows from several tables according\nto a condition. An <strong>INNER JOIN<\/strong> returns only combinations\nwith a match on both sides:<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb7\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb7-1\"><a href=\"#cb7-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">SELECT<\/span> k.name <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> kanalname, v.titel<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb7-2\"><a href=\"#cb7-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">FROM<\/span> kanal <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> k<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb7-3\"><a href=\"#cb7-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">JOIN<\/span> video <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> v <span class=\"kw\">ON<\/span> v.kanal_id <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> k.kanal_id<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb7-4\"><a href=\"#cb7-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">ORDER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">BY<\/span> k.kanal_id, v.video_id;<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The result contains three video rows, but <strong>not<\/strong>\n<code>Leerkanal<\/code>: it has no matching entry on the video side.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>LEFT JOIN<\/strong>, by contrast, includes all rows of the\nleft table even if nothing matches on the right. The missing right-hand\nvalues appear as <code>NULL<\/code>:<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb8\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb8-1\"><a href=\"#cb8-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">SELECT<\/span> k.name, v.titel<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb8-2\"><a href=\"#cb8-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">FROM<\/span> kanal <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> k<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb8-3\"><a href=\"#cb8-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">LEFT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">JOIN<\/span> video <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> v <span class=\"kw\">ON<\/span> v.kanal_id <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> k.kanal_id<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb8-4\"><a href=\"#cb8-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">ORDER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">BY<\/span> k.kanal_id, v.video_id;<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Here <code>Leerkanal<\/code> also appears, with <code>NULL<\/code> as\nthe title. ‘No matching video found’ does not therefore become ‘a video\nwith an empty title’. This, too, is a domain distinction.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A classic trap:<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb9\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb9-1\"><a href=\"#cb9-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">SELECT<\/span> k.name, <span class=\"fu\">COUNT<\/span>(<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> anzahl<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb9-2\"><a href=\"#cb9-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">FROM<\/span> kanal <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> k<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb9-3\"><a href=\"#cb9-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">LEFT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">JOIN<\/span> video <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> v <span class=\"kw\">ON<\/span> v.kanal_id <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> k.kanal_id<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb9-4\"><a href=\"#cb9-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">GROUP<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">BY<\/span> k.kanal_id, k.name;<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>For <code>Leerkanal<\/code>, this query counts <strong>1<\/strong>,\nbecause the result row created by the LEFT JOIN is counted as well. For\n‘number of existing videos’, the correct expression is\n<code>COUNT(v.video_id)<\/code>: here the aggregate counts only rows with\na non-null video ID and returns 0.<\/p>\n<p>A further trap is possible with an n:m join. If a video has several\ntags, the join returns several rows <strong>for the same video<\/strong>.\nAnyone who then adds up all <code>aufrufe<\/code> values may count the\nviews repeatedly. The solution is not automatically to apply\n<code>DISTINCT<\/code> blindly: first, the intended\n<strong>grain<\/strong> of the result must be defined in domain terms,\nsuch as ‘one row per video’, ‘one row per tag assignment’ or ‘one row\nper channel’. If needed, the data is first aggregated at this correct\nlevel and then joined further.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Filtering, grouping, NULL and three-valued logic<\/h2>\n<p>The most important aggregate functions are\n<strong><code>COUNT<\/code><\/strong> (count),\n<strong><code>SUM<\/code><\/strong> (sum),\n<strong><code>AVG<\/code><\/strong> (arithmetic mean),\n<strong><code>MIN<\/code><\/strong> and <strong><code>MAX<\/code><\/strong>.\nAn example:<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb10\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb10-1\"><a href=\"#cb10-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">SELECT<\/span> k.name,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb10-2\"><a href=\"#cb10-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>       <span class=\"fu\">COUNT<\/span>(v.video_id) <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> videozahl,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb10-3\"><a href=\"#cb10-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>       <span class=\"fu\">COALESCE<\/span>(<span class=\"fu\">SUM<\/span>(v.aufrufe), <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> gesamte_aufrufe<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb10-4\"><a href=\"#cb10-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">FROM<\/span> kanal <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> k<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb10-5\"><a href=\"#cb10-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">LEFT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">JOIN<\/span> video <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> v <span class=\"kw\">ON<\/span> v.kanal_id <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> k.kanal_id<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb10-6\"><a href=\"#cb10-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">GROUP<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">BY<\/span> k.kanal_id, k.name<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb10-7\"><a href=\"#cb10-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">ORDER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">BY<\/span> k.kanal_id;<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Channel<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">videozahl<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">gesamte_aufrufe<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Wissen<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">200<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Technik<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">200<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Leerkanal<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>For <code>Leerkanal<\/code>, <code>SUM(v.aufrufe)<\/code> without\nmatches is <strong><code>NULL<\/code><\/strong>. With\n<code>COALESCE(..., 0)<\/code>, this becomes 0 for the report. The\ndecision is plausible in domain terms, but is not correct for every\nmetric: an unknown measurement is not necessarily the same as zero.<\/p>\n<p><strong><code>NULL<\/code><\/strong> in SQL denotes the absence of a\nvalue, not the number 0 or the empty string. A normal equality test such\nas <code>spalte = NULL<\/code> does not yield the usual true/false\nresult. SQL has three possible outcomes for conditions: <strong>true,\nfalse and unknown<\/strong>; <code>IS NULL<\/code> or\n<code>IS NOT NULL<\/code> is needed here. Comparisons such as\n<code>NULL = NULL<\/code> are not simply ‘true’ either.<\/p>\n<p>The difference between <code>WHERE<\/code> and <code>HAVING<\/code> is\ncrucial:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>WHERE<\/code><\/strong> filters input rows before\ngrouping.<\/li>\n<li><strong><code>HAVING<\/code><\/strong> filters groups according to\ntheir aggregated values.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb11\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb11-1\"><a href=\"#cb11-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">SELECT<\/span> kanal_id, <span class=\"fu\">COUNT<\/span>(<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> zahl<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb11-2\"><a href=\"#cb11-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">FROM<\/span> video<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb11-3\"><a href=\"#cb11-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">WHERE<\/span> aufrufe <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">100<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb11-4\"><a href=\"#cb11-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">GROUP<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">BY<\/span> kanal_id<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb11-5\"><a href=\"#cb11-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">HAVING<\/span> <span class=\"fu\">COUNT<\/span>(<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>;<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>This query counts only videos with at least 100 views. This is\n<strong>not<\/strong> the same question as ‘Which channels have at least\n100 views in total?’. For that, one would calculate\n<code>SUM(aufrufe)<\/code> per channel and test it in\n<code>HAVING<\/code>. A small difference in wording can mean a completely\ndifferent result in domain terms.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Subqueries, EXISTS and common table expressions<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>subquery<\/strong> is an SQL query within another. With\n<strong><code>EXISTS<\/code><\/strong>, we ask whether at least one\nmatching record exists. This is particularly useful when the number of\nrelationships does not matter, only their existence.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb12\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb12-1\"><a href=\"#cb12-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">SELECT<\/span> v.video_id, v.titel<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb12-2\"><a href=\"#cb12-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">FROM<\/span> video <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> v<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb12-3\"><a href=\"#cb12-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">WHERE<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">EXISTS<\/span> (<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb12-4\"><a href=\"#cb12-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"kw\">SELECT<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb12-5\"><a href=\"#cb12-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"kw\">FROM<\/span> video_tag <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> vt<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb12-6\"><a href=\"#cb12-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"kw\">JOIN<\/span> tag <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> t <span class=\"kw\">ON<\/span> t.tag_id <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> vt.tag_id<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb12-7\"><a href=\"#cb12-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"kw\">WHERE<\/span> vt.video_id <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> v.video_id <span class=\"kw\">AND<\/span> t.name <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;Natur&#39;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb12-8\"><a href=\"#cb12-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb12-9\"><a href=\"#cb12-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">ORDER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">BY<\/span> v.video_id;<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>This returns videos 101 and 102, each exactly once.\n<code>EXISTS<\/code> asks whether there is a match; it does not multiply\nvideo rows like an ordinary join when several matching assignments\nexist.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>common table expression (CTE)<\/strong> is a named subquery\nincorporated into a larger query with <code>WITH<\/code>. It is a means\nof structuring a query, not necessarily a permanently stored table.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb13\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb13-1\"><a href=\"#cb13-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">WITH<\/span> kanal_aufrufe <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> (<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb13-2\"><a href=\"#cb13-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"kw\">SELECT<\/span> kanal_id, <span class=\"fu\">SUM<\/span>(aufrufe) <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> summe<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb13-3\"><a href=\"#cb13-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"kw\">FROM<\/span> video<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb13-4\"><a href=\"#cb13-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"kw\">GROUP<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">BY<\/span> kanal_id<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb13-5\"><a href=\"#cb13-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb13-6\"><a href=\"#cb13-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">SELECT<\/span> k.name, a.summe<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb13-7\"><a href=\"#cb13-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">FROM<\/span> kanal <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> k<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb13-8\"><a href=\"#cb13-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">JOIN<\/span> kanal_aufrufe <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> a <span class=\"kw\">ON<\/span> a.kanal_id <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> k.kanal_id<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb13-9\"><a href=\"#cb13-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">ORDER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">BY<\/span> a.summe <span class=\"kw\">DESC<\/span>, k.kanal_id;<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Here ‘Wissen’ and ‘Technik’ appear with 200 views each.\n<code>Leerkanal<\/code> is absent because of the inner join; this could\nbe changed by a LEFT JOIN and a domain-appropriate way of handling null\nvalues.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Important:<\/strong> A CTE does not automatically make a query\nfaster. Depending on the database system, version and structure, it may\nbe integrated into a plan or processed separately. The form is primarily\nan aid to comprehensibility and to establishing a clearly named\nintermediate level.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Window functions: comparing without collapsing rows<\/h2>\n<p>Grouping with <code>GROUP BY<\/code>, for example, condenses two\nvideos of a channel into <strong>one channel row<\/strong>. A\n<strong>window function<\/strong>, by contrast, can add a comparison\n<strong>for each individual video row<\/strong> without first collapsing\nthe rows.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb14\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb14-1\"><a href=\"#cb14-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">SELECT<\/span> kanal_id, video_id, titel, aufrufe,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb14-2\"><a href=\"#cb14-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>       <span class=\"fu\">ROW_NUMBER<\/span>() <span class=\"kw\">OVER<\/span> (<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb14-3\"><a href=\"#cb14-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>           <span class=\"kw\">PARTITION<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">BY<\/span> kanal_id<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb14-4\"><a href=\"#cb14-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>           <span class=\"kw\">ORDER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">BY<\/span> aufrufe <span class=\"kw\">DESC<\/span>, video_id<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb14-5\"><a href=\"#cb14-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>       ) <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> rang_im_kanal<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb14-6\"><a href=\"#cb14-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">FROM<\/span> video<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb14-7\"><a href=\"#cb14-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">ORDER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">BY<\/span> kanal_id, rang_im_kanal;<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p><code>PARTITION BY<\/code> separates the calculation by channel;\n<code>ORDER BY<\/code> inside the window specifies the ranking. For the\nknowledge channel (Wissen), ‘Bäume’ receives rank 1 and ‘Flüsse’ rank 2.\n‘Netze’ has rank 1 in its channel. <code>ROW_NUMBER<\/code> counts\nstrictly consecutively; <code>RANK<\/code> may assign shared ranks to\nequal values and skip subsequent numbers.<\/p>\n<p>For running sums, the <strong>window frame<\/strong> is also\nimportant. Unless a frame is specified explicitly, product- and\nclause-dependent defaults apply. For example, in\n<strong>PostgreSQL<\/strong>, with\n<code>SUM(aufrufe) OVER (ORDER BY veroeffentlicht_am)<\/code>, the\ndefault frame also includes <em>peer rows<\/em> with identical\npublication dates; a running sum may therefore increase abruptly when\ndates tie. If a running sum proceeding row by row is intended, one can\nuse an explicit frame such as\n<code>ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW<\/code> and a\nunique sort order by date <strong>and<\/strong> <code>video_id<\/code>.\nWithout <code>ORDER BY<\/code>, on the other hand, the window calculation\ntypically covers the whole partition. The behaviour must be\ndistinguished according to the selected window function and the\nparticular database system. Window functions are evaluated later than\n<code>WHERE<\/code> in logical SQL processing; an enclosing subquery or\nCTE is therefore typically used to filter a calculated rank.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When useful?<\/strong> Rankings, comparisons with group\naverages, time series and analyses containing individual entries and\ntheir group context. <strong>When unnecessary?<\/strong> A simple list of\nfive titles does not require a complex window construction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Query plans and indexes: why correct SQL can still be\nslow<\/h2>\n<p>An <strong>index<\/strong> is an additional search structure, often a\nvariant of a balanced B-tree. It makes certain search and sorting paths\nmore efficient, but requires storage of its own and must be updated when\nchanges occur. The <strong>query optimiser<\/strong> (<em>query\nplanner<\/em>) estimates whether an index, a table scan or a combination\nof several methods is suitable for the query.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>SQL: Videos from channel 1, newest first\n               |\n               v\n           Query planner\n             /      \\\n            v        v\n     Table scan    Index search on (kanal_id, date)\n            \\        /\n             v      v\n               Result<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A common combined access pattern is:<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb16\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb16-1\"><a href=\"#cb16-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">SELECT<\/span> video_id, titel<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb16-2\"><a href=\"#cb16-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">FROM<\/span> video<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb16-3\"><a href=\"#cb16-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">WHERE<\/span> kanal_id <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb16-4\"><a href=\"#cb16-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">ORDER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">BY<\/span> veroeffentlicht_am <span class=\"kw\">DESC<\/span>, video_id <span class=\"kw\">DESC<\/span>;<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>One possible index for this is:<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb17\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb17-1\"><a href=\"#cb17-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">CREATE<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">INDEX<\/span> ix_video_kanal_datum_id<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb17-2\"><a href=\"#cb17-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">ON<\/span> video (kanal_id, veroeffentlicht_am <span class=\"kw\">DESC<\/span>, video_id <span class=\"kw\">DESC<\/span>);<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The leftmost column <code>kanal_id<\/code> helps select a specific\nchannel; the subsequent index columns fit the desired sort order.\n<strong>Order in composite indexes matters<\/strong>: an index on\n<code>(kanal_id, veroeffentlicht_am)<\/code> is not equally useful for\nevery query that filters purely by date. The exact behaviour depends on\nthe system, the distribution and the query plan.<\/p>\n<p>To illustrate: a table of 1,000,000 videos has 1,000 channels with\n1,000 videos per channel, <em>uniformly distributed in the model<\/em>. A\nfilter on one channel reduces the candidates on average from 1,000,000\nto 1,000 – that is, to one thousandth. This does <strong>not<\/strong>\nmean that an index is exactly a thousand times faster in practice: an\nindex lookup itself takes time; data may be cached; indexes are not\nadvantageous for every sort order or for many simultaneous changes.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Selectivity<\/strong> describes, in simplified terms, how\nstrongly a predicate restricts the data set. A common status field such\nas <code>sichtbar = 1<\/code> might return almost all rows and does not\non its own justify an index. <strong>Statistics<\/strong> on data\ndistributions help the planner estimate the row count. Outdated\nstatistics can lead to poor decisions.<\/p>\n<p>With <strong><code>EXPLAIN<\/code><\/strong>, PostgreSQL can display\nthe planned access method; <strong><code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE<\/code><\/strong>\nactually executes the query and measures real parts of its processing.\nThis is not harmless for statements that write data – those statements\nare actually executed too. PostgreSQL documentation presents the\ncontrolled teaching pattern\n<code>BEGIN; EXPLAIN ANALYZE ...; ROLLBACK;<\/code>: the transaction\nreverses the database change, but is no substitute for carefully\nchecking other possible side effects. A theoretical planner cost value\nis not directly a time in milliseconds.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Index maintenance is a trade-off:<\/strong> reads may become\nfaster, while writes require additional index updates. More indexes\nconsume storage and may burden backups or migrations. For small tables,\nthe database may deliberately choose a full scan; this is not\nautomatically an error.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Transactions: making correct changes is a task of its\nown<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>transaction<\/strong> combines logically related changes\ninto one unit. <code>BEGIN<\/code>, <code>COMMIT<\/code> and\n<code>ROLLBACK<\/code> denote the beginning, confirmed completion and\nreversal of uncommitted changes. The <strong>ACID<\/strong> properties\nare familiar from <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L5<\/a>:\natomicity, consistency, isolation and durability.<\/p>\n<p>Imagine two processes wanting to change the view count of a video at\nthe same time. Both read the old value 100 and calculate 101. If both\nsimply write back ‘101’, one increment is lost (<strong>lost\nupdate<\/strong>). An atomic change directly to the current database\nvalue can prevent this specific error:<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb18\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb18-1\"><a href=\"#cb18-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">UPDATE<\/span> video<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb18-2\"><a href=\"#cb18-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">SET<\/span> aufrufe <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> aufrufe <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb18-3\"><a href=\"#cb18-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">WHERE<\/span> video_id <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">101<\/span>;<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>For more complex rules, an <strong>optimistic concurrency\ncheck<\/strong> using a version column may help. A change is only\npermitted if the previously read version still applies. If no row is\nchanged, the application must deliberately handle the conflict instead\nof ignoring it. <strong>Locks<\/strong> and higher <strong>isolation\nlevels<\/strong> are further options, but they often increase waiting\ntimes, conflicts or the need to retry.<\/p>\n<p>Example: two users reserve the same last place. The database must\nensure that both confirmations cannot simultaneously appear to be valid.\nSimply ‘checking first whether it is still free’ outside a controlled\ntransaction is insufficient under concurrency. The appropriate rule may\nbe a unique key, an atomic conditional write or a transaction with a\nsuitably chosen isolation level.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Isolation levels<\/strong> are not a universal set of switches\nwith identical behaviour in all systems. PostgreSQL’s\n<code>READ COMMITTED<\/code>, <code>REPEATABLE READ<\/code> and\n<code>SERIALIZABLE<\/code> have documented properties; under serialisable\nprocessing, transactions can be aborted and must be retried at\napplication level. SQLite coordinates concurrent access differently,\nincluding through journal or write-ahead logging modes and limited\nconcurrent writing capabilities. Migration from SQLite to PostgreSQL is\ntherefore more than copying tables.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Secure queries, user separation and data protection<\/h2>\n<p>An SQL statement is not automatically secure simply because it works\ncorrectly in domain terms. <strong>SQL injection<\/strong> occurs when\nuntrusted input unintentionally becomes SQL syntax.\n<strong>Parameterised queries<\/strong> are considered the most important\ndefence: the SQL structure remains fixed, and values are bound\nseparately through the database interface. Placeholder syntax and\nprogramming interfaces vary by driver.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Secure principle:\nFixed SQL:  SELECT video_id FROM video WHERE kanal_id = ?\nValue:      1   (bound separately through the database interface)\n\nInsecure principle:\nConcatenating characters supplied by the user\nwith an SQL string in an uncontrolled way.<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Placeholders are intended for <strong>values<\/strong>, not\nautomatically for arbitrary table names, column names or\n<code>ASC<\/code>/<code>DESC<\/code>. For dynamic sorting options, use a\nsmall, verified set built into the application. Even correct parameter\nbinding does not replace <strong>authorisation<\/strong>: a user could\nstill supply the ID of someone else’s record. The server must therefore\nenforce access rules.<\/p>\n<p>In a service with several customers, one often speaks of\n<strong>tenants<\/strong>. A simple shared table with\n<code>mandant_id<\/code> is technically possible, but error-prone if the\ntenant filter is forgotten in even a single query. Alternatives include\nseparate databases or database schemas; all variants have costs,\noperational consequences and security limitations. In PostgreSQL, under\nsuitable conditions, <strong>row-level security (RLS)<\/strong> can\nprovide additional database rules. Simply activating it does not replace\nchecking the entire permissions and role model.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Personal data<\/strong> should be considered not only when it\nis collected but also during modelling: what information is necessary,\nhow long is it stored, who may see it and how are correction and\ndeletion carried out? Indexes, logs, export files and backups may also\ncontain such data. Deletion routines must realistically account for\nretention obligations, backup cycles and technical recovery; this\nchapter is not legal advice.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Schema changes: a database is not finished after its first\ndesign<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>schema<\/strong> describes tables, columns, rules and other\ndatabase objects. A <strong>migration<\/strong> changes this schema or\ntransforms the data already stored. Such changes require version\nmanagement, testability and a recovery path in case of error.<\/p>\n<p>Suppose <code>video.titel<\/code> is to be supplemented in future with\nan editable short name. Direct renaming can render an older program\nversion unusable. For long-running online services, a staged approach is\noften useful:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>1. Add the new column; continue supporting the old one\n                     |\n                     v\n2. The application can initially read/write both forms\n                     |\n                     v\n3. Update existing records in a controlled manner\n                     |\n                     v\n4. Switch the new version exclusively to the new form\n                     |\n                     v\n5. Remove the old column only after checks and rollback planning<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This is called <strong>expand-and-contract<\/strong>. It is not a\nmandatory method for every application: for a purely offline tool, a\nplanned, tested one-off transition may be easier. For a publicly used\nservice, however, such intermediate states avoid some compatibility and\ndowntime problems.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>backup<\/strong> before a schema change does not\nautomatically protect against every error. One must also test whether it\ncan be restored, whether the application is compatible with the restored\ndata structure and which changes are lost between backup and failure.\nLarge tables can be placed under noticeable strain when columns are\naltered or indexes are created; some operations block writes or create\nadditional storage requirements. The specific properties depend on the\ndatabase system and SQL statement.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three illustrative architecture examples<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"example-a-private-local-media-catalogue\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example A – Private\nlocal media catalogue<\/h3>\n<p>A local catalogue organises video and image files with titles, tags\nand search criteria. In small, single-user operation, SQLite may be a\nsimple choice: an embedded database, SQL queries, transactions and no\nseparate database server administration.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Desktop interface\n        |\n        v\nMedia catalogue logic\n        |\n        v\nSQLite database ----&gt; Indexes for channel / tag / date\n        |\n        v\nFile paths to locally stored media<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Advantages:<\/strong> straightforward operation, easy\nportability, no network service required. <strong>Limitations:<\/strong>\na local file path does not automatically remain valid permanently;\nremoved or renamed files create orphaned references. The database and\nmedia files require a coordinated backup strategy. With several\nconcurrent writers, the demands on coordination and storage media\nincrease. A cloud cluster would be unnecessary for the single-user\ncatalogue described here.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"example-b-small-public-web-service\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example B – Small public web\nservice<\/h3>\n<p>A service offers several signed-in users a search function over their\nown collections. A relational server DBMS such as PostgreSQL may be\nsuitable when several clients write concurrently, access permissions\nhave to be separated and reliable network operation is required.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser\n   |\n   v\nWeb API with authentication and authorisation\n   |\n   v\nApplication logic (parameterised SQL queries)\n   |\n   v\nPostgreSQL: tables, constraints, indexes, transactions\n   |\n   +--&gt; Backups / restore rehearsals\n   +--&gt; Monitoring / migrations<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Advantages:<\/strong> central data storage and an architecture\ndesigned for concurrent users. <strong>Costs:<\/strong> server operation,\nsecurity updates, access protection, backups, incident handling and\npossibly usage-dependent hosting fees. Making databases directly\naccessible on the public internet would not be a necessary feature of a\nweb service and increases the attack surface.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"example-c-sensor-readings-and-time-series\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example C – Sensor\nreadings and time series<\/h3>\n<p>A device sends temperature readings regularly. A useful logical\nrecord includes at least <code>sensor_id<\/code>, timestamp, measured\nvalue and unit, or a clearly documented system of units.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Sensor -&gt; Ingestion service -&gt; validated timestamps / values\n                              |\n                              v\n                    Measurement table by sensor and time\n                              |\n                              v\n                    Time-window aggregates / charts<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Modelling questions:<\/strong> can a measurement arrive late?\nAre repeated transmissions allowed? What does a missing measurement\nmean? May the sensor be recalibrated retrospectively? What time range is\nretained? An index on <code>(sensor_id, zeitpunkt)<\/code> can support\nrepeated time-range queries; with very large data volumes, partitioning,\naggregation and retention policies also become relevant. These measures\nraise new maintenance questions and are not essential for a few hundred\nreadings.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two small, verifiable calculations<\/h2>\n<h3\nid=\"example-1-duplicate-values-caused-by-the-wrong-join-grain\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example 1\n– Duplicate values caused by the wrong join grain<\/h3>\n<p>Suppose video 101 has 120 views and receives two tags. A join of\n<code>video<\/code> with <code>video_tag<\/code> then creates two result\nrows for this video. A blind <code>SUM(video.aufrufe)<\/code> over this\njoined list returns <strong>240<\/strong> instead of\n<strong>120<\/strong>. This is not a calculation error by the database\nsystem; the query supplied the same video’s view count twice for\nsummation. The remedy is a domain-correct result basis per video or an\nappropriate aggregation beforehand.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"example-2-the-difference-between-count-and-countspalte\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example\n2 – The difference between <code>COUNT(*)<\/code> and\n<code>COUNT(spalte)<\/code><\/h3>\n<p><code>Leerkanal<\/code> has no video. Its <code>LEFT JOIN<\/code> still\nproduces exactly one result row in which all video columns are\n<code>NULL<\/code>. <code>COUNT(*)<\/code> counts rows and therefore gives\n<strong>1<\/strong>; <code>COUNT(v.video_id)<\/code> counts only present\nIDs and gives <strong>0<\/strong>. The right choice derives from the\nquestion, not from the superficial similarity of the two\nexpressions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Decision guide<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question / initial situation<\/th>\n<th>Often useful decision<\/th>\n<th>Error to avoid<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Small local data store<\/td>\n<td>Text file or SQLite, depending on requirements<\/td>\n<td>Forcing a separate database server without need<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Many related, changing data<\/td>\n<td>Relationships, keys and rules in the data model<\/td>\n<td>Maintaining the same names uncontrolled in many rows<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Several tags per video<\/td>\n<td>Separate assignment table<\/td>\n<td><code>tag1<\/code>, <code>tag2<\/code>, <code>tag3<\/code> with a fixed\nlimit<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sums by category<\/td>\n<td>First determine the correct grain, then group<\/td>\n<td>Overlooking double counting due to joins<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Search becomes slow<\/td>\n<td>Inspect real queries and <code>EXPLAIN<\/code>, index\nselectively<\/td>\n<td>Indexing every data type and every column as a precaution<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Several users make changes simultaneously<\/td>\n<td>Transactions, constraints and a conflict strategy<\/td>\n<td>Read–modify–write without concurrency checks<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Public application API<\/td>\n<td>Parameter binding, authorisation, minimal database privileges<\/td>\n<td>Constructing SQL from inputs; treating an ID alone as\npermission<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data model changes<\/td>\n<td>Planned migration, tests, backup and rollback path<\/td>\n<td>Deleting columns without considering old software versions<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Historical measurements<\/td>\n<td>Time-based data structure and retention rules<\/td>\n<td>Storing only the most recent value and expecting a time series<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S17\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and connection to the rest of the course<\/h2>\n<p>A good data model translates domain rules into verifiable structures.\n<strong>Keys and relationships<\/strong> enforce identity and\nassociation; <strong>normalisation<\/strong> prevents many data\ninconsistencies; <strong>SQL<\/strong> describes the desired data sets;\n<strong>joins and aggregates<\/strong> require an understanding of result\ngrain; <strong>indexes<\/strong> serve specific search patterns and cost\nstorage and write work; <strong>transactions and permissions<\/strong>\nprotect correctness and data access. None of these techniques\nautomatically makes the system secure, fast or easy to maintain.<\/p>\n<p>M10-L2 concludes the <strong>eight optional Bachelor elective topics\nM8-L1–M10-L2<\/strong>. The core computer science course M1-L1–M6-L7\nforms its own reading path. For deeper mathematical study, University\nM1-L1 offers sets and propositional logic, School (German-language)\nM1-L18 covers functions, and University M2-L1 covers discrete structures\nand combinatorics. Computer Science M7-L1 subsequently examines models\nand scientific reasoning.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S18\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Placement in the course-wide reading paths<\/h2>\n<p>This is an <strong>optional Bachelor elective lesson<\/strong>, not an\nadditional main course lesson. Among other connections, it leads to <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L4 in the Master core<\/a>. Further\nroutes: <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;page=path&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">course-wide reading\npaths<\/a> and <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;page=index&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">course index<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S19\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source check:<\/strong> 04.11.2026<\/p>\n<p><strong>Check date:<\/strong> 04.11.2026. Preference was given to\nprimary documentation sources. Vendor documentation describes the\nrespective product and should not be read as universal SQL behaviour\nwithout verification.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>PostgreSQL<\/strong>, <em>Constraints<\/em>:\n<code>PRIMARY KEY<\/code>, <code>FOREIGN KEY<\/code>, <code>UNIQUE<\/code>,\n<code>CHECK<\/code>, referential integrity and delete actions. <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-constraints.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-constraints.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>PostgreSQL<\/strong>, <em>Table Expressions<\/em>: joins,\n<code>WHERE<\/code>, grouping and <code>HAVING<\/code>. <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/queries-table-expressions.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/queries-table-expressions.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>PostgreSQL<\/strong>, <em>Window Functions<\/em>:\n<code>OVER<\/code>, <code>PARTITION BY<\/code>, rankings and evaluation\norder. <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial-window.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial-window.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>PostgreSQL<\/strong>, <em>WITH Queries (CTEs)<\/em>:\nstructuring large queries and system-specific optimisation. <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/queries-with.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/queries-with.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>PostgreSQL<\/strong>, <em>Using EXPLAIN<\/em>: query plans,\nmeasurement and limitations. <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/using-explain.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/using-explain.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>PostgreSQL<\/strong>, <em>Examining Index Usage<\/em>:\nassessing indexes using real queries and statistics. <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/indexes-examine.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/indexes-examine.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>PostgreSQL<\/strong>, <em>Transaction Isolation<\/em>:\nisolation levels and concurrent transactions. <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/transaction-iso.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/transaction-iso.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>SQLite<\/strong>, <em>Foreign Key Support<\/em>: enabled\nconstraints and special considerations for foreign keys. <a\nhref=\"https://www.sqlite.org/foreignkeys.html\">https://www.sqlite.org/foreignkeys.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>SQLite<\/strong>, <em>Isolation In SQLite<\/em>: rollback\njournal and write-ahead logging. <a\nhref=\"https://www.sqlite.org/isolation.html\">https://www.sqlite.org/isolation.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>SQLite<\/strong>, <em>Transactions<\/em>: beginning a\ntransaction, commit and rollback. <a\nhref=\"https://www.sqlite.org/lang_transaction.html\">https://www.sqlite.org/lang_transaction.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>OWASP Cheat Sheet Series<\/strong>, <em>SQL Injection\nPrevention<\/em>: parameter binding, permitted structural parameters and\nminimal privileges. <a\nhref=\"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html\">https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>PostgreSQL<\/strong>, <em>Row Security Policies<\/em>:\nrow-level data access. <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-rowsecurity.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-rowsecurity.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further reading paths:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L5 – Data and databases<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L1 – A database on a server<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L3 – Partitioning and sharding<\/a>,\n<a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L5 – Transactions and\ndistributed correctness<\/a>, <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L1\n– Discrete computer science<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L2 – Data analysis<\/a>.<\/p>\n<\/article>"};
